/* ==========================================================================
   Star questions, Dr. Lahoud's half of the exam (20 items): the item types most
   likely to appear on the written exam. About half are case items that give a
   prescription, a label and a stock bottle as short labelled lines (the lab
   format: legal and safe? transcribed appropriately? product matches?), then
   ask which errors are present or whether there are none. The rest are
   category rules with date arithmetic, substitution, counseling and the Week 14
   DEENT lab (QuEST, acne, ophthalmic sorting, glaucoma prescription review,
   scaly dermatoses and psoriasis).
   Sources: Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf (slide N = PDF page), EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf
   (13 stations), the 8/24 transcript (T) for fill-date, stock-expiry and PMR
   remarks, and for DEENT the verified items already in q7 to q11.
   Every case item was recomputed: days between dates, one year after fill
   versus the stock bottle's month-end date, and 10 days after mixing.
   Facts only in a recording are marked (T) and set source 'both'.
   Notes: (1) the EOOR key words the CII limit as more than 1 month, slide 22
   gives 30 days; the items here use dates that give the same answer either way
   except where the 30 days is computed, and say so. (2) sl11 names ibuprofen
   with the 3200 mg/day maximum; the key's station 7 gives the 3200 mg/day
   maximum without naming the drug.
   ========================================================================== */


QUESTIONS.push(
{id:'sl01', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-case-metformin',
 tags:['star','EOOR','PMR','label date'], source:'both',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Prescription (written, dangerous drug, manually signed; prescriber name, address and phone present): issued 11/16/2026 | Daniel Reyes; no address or date of birth (DOB) on it, both in the patient medication record (PMR) | metformin extended-release (ER) 500 mg tablets, #30, 1 tablet by mouth once daily with the evening meal | Indication: type 2 diabetes | Refills: 11. Label: metformin ER 500 mg tablets, 1 tablet once daily with the evening meal, #30, refills 11, Use Before 11/21/2027. Stock bottle: metformin immediate-release (IR) 500 mg tablets, Exp 03/2027. Which errors are present? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Patient address and DOB missing from the prescription', correct:false, why:'For a dangerous (non-controlled) drug the address may be missing when it is retrievable from the PMR, and the DOB is not required on the prescription if the PMR holds it (slide 16; T). The same omission would invalidate a CIII–V prescription (slide 19), so the rule depends on the drug class.'},
  {t:'Incorrect formulation filled', correct:true, why:'Product verification checks formulation, including ER versus IR (slide 36). The prescription and label say ER but the bottle is IR, a dispensing error on slide 38 that is fixed by replacing the product and relabeling before dispensing (slide 39). EOOR station 10 is the same error.'},
  {t:'Inappropriate number of refills prescribed', correct:false, why:'Dangerous drugs have no refill limit and the script lasts 1 year (slide 22), so 11 refills is allowed. Choosing this applies the CIII–V limit of 5 refills or the CII limit of 0 refills to a dangerous drug, mixing up neighboring categories.'},
  {t:'Prescription is expired', correct:false, why:'A dangerous-drug prescription is valid for 1 year after issuance (slide 22). It was issued 11/16/2026 and is filled 11/21/2026, so it is 5 days old. Choosing this applies the 6-month CIII–V or 30-day CII expiration to the wrong category.'},
  {t:'“Use Before” date assigned on label is incorrect', correct:true, why:'The label date is the earlier of one year after fill (11/21/2027) and the stock bottle expiration (slide 25). 03/2027 is earlier, and a month-and-year date runs to the last day of the month, so the label should read 3/31/2027 (EOOR station 10: 1/2027 becomes 1/31/2027). This is a dispensing error.'},
  {t:'Incorrect strength filled', correct:false, why:'The prescription and the bottle both show 500 mg, so the strength matches (slide 36, item 2). The mismatch is ER versus IR, which slide 38 lists as a separate formulation error. Choosing this confuses strength with formulation.'}],
 teach:'Work the three review steps in order. Step 1, legal and safe: for a dangerous drug the patient address and DOB may come from the PMR, refills have no limit and the script lasts 1 year. Step 2, transcribed appropriately: compare drug, strength, form, directions, quantity and refills with the prescription. Step 3, product match: read the stock bottle for drug, strength, formulation (ER versus IR) and expiration. The Use Before date is the earlier of one year after the fill date and the stock expiration, and a month-and-year stock date means the last day of that month (3/2027 becomes 3/31/2027). Quick method: write fill date plus one year, write the stock date as a month end, and choose the earlier. Dispensing errors are fixed with the technician by replacing the product and relabeling.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 16, 19, 22, 25, 36–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slides 1–2 (station 10); 8/24 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl02', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-no-error-cv',
 tags:['star','EOOR','no errors','controlled'], source:'both',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Prescription (written, manually signed): issued 9/28/2026 | Priya Nair, address and date of birth (DOB) on it | prescriber’s name, address, phone and DEA number present | alprazolam 0.5 mg tablets, #30 (thirty), 1 tablet by mouth three times daily as needed for anxiety | Refills: 5. Label: alprazolam 0.5 mg tablets, #30, 1 tablet by mouth three times daily as needed for anxiety, refills 5, Use Before 11/21/2027. Stock bottle: alprazolam 0.5 mg tablets, Exp 08/2028. What is the best assessment?',
 options:[
  {t:'Prescribing error: 5 refills exceed the CIII–V limit', correct:false, why:'Schedule III–V prescriptions allow up to 5 refills within 6 months (slides 22, 27), and alprazolam is Schedule IV (T). Five refills is at the limit, not over it. Choosing this misremembers the limit, or applies the CII rule of 0 refills.'},
  {t:'Dispensing error: Use Before date must match the stock bottle', correct:false, why:'The label date is the earlier of one year after fill and the stock expiration (slide 25). One year after 11/21/2026 is 11/21/2027, which is earlier than 08/2028, so the label is right. Choosing this ignores the one-year limit on vials filled from a stock bottle.'},
  {t:'No errors and safe to dispense as is', correct:true, why:'Step 1 passes: 54 days old (9/28 to 11/21: 2 + 31 + 21), inside the 6-month CIII–V limit; address, DOB, DEA number and the quantity written as a word are present, and 5 refills is the maximum. Steps 2 and 3 pass (slide 25 date; EOOR stations 5 and 11 are the no-error stations). A PMP check before a benzodiazepine (slide 21) is a workflow step, not an error.'},
  {t:'Prescribing error: the prescription is older than 30 days', correct:false, why:'The 30-day limit belongs to CII prescriptions (slide 22); a CIII–V prescription is valid for 6 months. At 54 days this prescription is expired only if it were a CII. Choosing this reveals mixing up the expiration of neighboring categories.'}],
 teach:'A no-error case is built from near-misses that are actually allowed: a refill count exactly at the limit, a script that is old but still inside its category’s window, and a stock expiration that is later than one year after the fill date. Check the category first (alprazolam is Schedule IV (T), so address, DOB, DEA number and the written quantity are required on the prescription even if in the PMR), then the dates: 6 months for CIII–V, 30 days for CII, 1 year for dangerous drugs. Then copy-check the label against the prescription and read the stock bottle. Only say an error exists when you can name the slide 38 item and its step. Controlled drugs in the opioid, benzodiazepine, barbiturate and carisoprodol groups also need a PMP history check before dispensing (slide 21).',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 19, 21, 22, 25, 27, 38; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slides 1–2 (stations 5 and 11); 8/24 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl03', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-case-cii',
 tags:['star','EOOR','CII','controlled'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Prescription (written on a Texas State Board of Pharmacy official form, manually signed; prescriber name, address, phone and DEA number present): issued 10/15/2026 | Kevin Brandt, address and date of birth on it | oxycodone 5 mg tablets, #30 (thirty), 1 tablet by mouth every 6 hours as needed for pain | Refills: 1. Label: oxycodone 5 mg tablets, #30, 1 tablet every 6 hours as needed for pain, refills 1, Use Before 11/21/2027. Stock bottle: oxycodone 5 mg tablets, Exp 06/2028. Which errors are present? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Number of refills transcribed incorrectly onto label', correct:false, why:'The label shows 1 refill and so does the prescription, so the label copies it correctly (slide 34, item 5). The refill is wrong at the source: it is a prescribing error, not a transcription error. Choosing this blames the label for the prescriber’s mistake.'},
  {t:'Prescription is expired', correct:true, why:'A CII prescription expires 30 days after issuance (slide 22). Issued 10/15/2026, the 30 days ended 11/14/2026; the fill date 11/21/2026 is day 37 (16 days left in October + 21). This is a prescribing error, fixed by contacting the prescriber for a new prescription (EOOR station 13).'},
  {t:'“Use Before” date assigned on label is incorrect', correct:false, why:'One year after 11/21/2026 is 11/21/2027, which is earlier than the stock expiration 06/2028 (6/30/2028), so the label date follows the one-year rule correctly (slide 25). Choosing this means the stock date was used without comparing it to the one-year limit.'},
  {t:'Inappropriate number of refills prescribed', correct:true, why:'Schedule II prescriptions allow 0 refills (slide 22), so a prescribed refill is a prescribing error that needs a call to the provider (EOOR station 13: CII cannot have refills). Choosing the 5-refill or unlimited answer for this category would mix up CIII–V or dangerous drugs.'},
  {t:'DEA number required but missing', correct:false, why:'The stem shows the DEA number present. A missing DEA number is the other error at EOOR stations 6 and 13 (contact the prescriber), but it is not an error here. Choosing it means the controlled-drug prescriber requirements were assumed, not read from the case.'},
  {t:'Written CII not on the official Texas State Board of Pharmacy form', correct:false, why:'A written CII must be on a TSBP-issued official form and manually signed (slide 20), and the stem says it is on the official form with a manual signature. Choosing this applies a correct rule to a prescription that already meets it.'}],
 teach:'For a CII, the two limits students overlook are 30 days (not 6 months) and 0 refills. Compute the days from the date of issuance to the fill date; any count above 30 is expired, so the script goes back to the prescriber, and a refill written on a CII is also fixed by calling the provider. Both are prescribing errors, so nothing the technician does can correct them. A written CII also needs the official TSBP form, a manual signature (no stamp), the quantity as a number and a word, and the pharmacist records the fill date and Rx number on the prescription (slide 20). A label that faithfully copies a wrong prescription is not a transcription error. The EOOR key words the CII limit as more than 1 month; slide 22 gives 30 days.',
 note:'EOOR station 13 words the CII expiration as more than 1 month; slide 22 gives 30 days. At 37 days both readings call the prescription expired.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 20, 22, 25, 34, 38; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slide 2 (station 13)'},

{id:'sl04', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-case-lasa',
 tags:['star','EOOR','LASA','label date'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Electronic prescription (dangerous drug, complete): issued 11/19/2026 | Nina Alvarez | escitalopram 10 mg tablets, #30, 1 tablet by mouth once daily | Indication: depression | Refills: 5 | substitution permitted. Label: escitalopram 10 mg tablets, #30, 1 tablet by mouth twice daily, refills 5, Use Before 9/30/2027. Stock bottle: citalopram 10 mg tablets, Exp 09/2027. Which errors are present? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Incorrect strength filled', correct:false, why:'Both the prescription and the bottle show 10 mg, so the strength matches (slide 36, item 2). The error is the drug, not the number, and slide 38 keeps strength and drug as separate dispensing errors. Choosing this is the confusion between a look-alike name and a different dose.'},
  {t:'Directions transcribed incorrectly onto label', correct:true, why:'The prescription says once daily and the label says twice daily. Slide 38 lists directions transcribed incorrectly under transcribing errors, and slide 39 says to correct the entry in the pharmacy system and regenerate the label (EOOR station 2: daily written, bid on the label).'},
  {t:'“Use Before” date assigned on label is incorrect', correct:false, why:'One year after 11/21/2026 is 11/21/2027, and the stock expiration 09/2027 is earlier, so the label should read the month end, 9/30/2027, which it does (slide 25; EOOR station 10). Choosing this treats a correct month-end date as wrong.'},
  {t:'Incorrect drug filled', correct:true, why:'The label correctly names escitalopram, but the bottle holds citalopram, a look-alike sound-alike pair. The bottle does not match the product (slide 36, item 1; slide 37), a dispensing error fixed by replacing the product and relabeling (slide 39; EOOR stations 1 and 8 follow the same fix).'},
  {t:'Number of refills transcribed incorrectly onto label', correct:false, why:'The prescription and label both say 5 refills, so the refills were transcribed correctly (slide 34, item 5). Choosing this means the wrong item on slide 38 was matched to a label that has a different error, the directions.'},
  {t:'Prescribing error: frequency too high; call the prescriber', correct:false, why:'The prescriber wrote once daily, which is not too high. The twice-daily frequency appears only on the label, so the fault is in transcribing (slide 38). Choosing this sends a transcription mistake to the prescriber instead of correcting it with the technician.'}],
 teach:'Locate each mismatch at the step where it first appears. Compare the prescription with the label: a difference in drug, directions or refills is a transcribing error, corrected in the pharmacy system with regenerated labels. Compare the label with the bottle: a difference in drug, strength, formulation or quantity is a dispensing error, corrected by replacing the product and relabeling. Only a fault in the prescription itself (expired, unsafe dose, missing DEA number, refills on a CII, insufficient prescribed quantity) is a prescribing error that goes to the prescriber. Citalopram and escitalopram share a 10 mg tablet, so strength does not separate them; read the drug name on the bottle. Month-and-year stock dates become the last day of the month (9/2027 becomes 9/30/2027).',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 25, 34, 36–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slides 1–2 (stations 1, 2, 8, 10)'},

{id:'sl05', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-brand-stamp',
 tags:['star','EOOR','substitution'], source:'both',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Prescription (written, dangerous drug, manually signed; patient and prescriber information complete): issued 11/18/2026 | Tom Hale | Bactrim double strength (DS) tablets, #20, 1 tablet by mouth twice daily for 10 days | Indication: purulent skin and soft tissue infection | Refills: 0 | a rubber stamp on the form reads “BRAND MEDICALLY NECESSARY.” Label: sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim DS 800/160 mg tablets, #20, 1 tablet twice daily for 10 days, refills 1, Use Before 11/21/2027. Stock bottle: sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim single strength (SS) 400/80 mg tablets, Exp 05/2028. Which errors are present? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Incorrect drug filled: generic filled although brand was requested', correct:false, why:'A rubber stamp does not legally prevent generic substitution on a written prescription; the prescriber must handwrite “Brand Necessary” or “Brand Medically Necessary” (slide 24; T). Choosing this treats any brand wording as binding, the opposite of EOOR station 2, where a valid request existed.'},
  {t:'Incorrect strength filled', correct:true, why:'The prescription and label say DS 800/160 mg, but the bottle is SS 400/80 mg. Strength is verified in step 3 (slide 36) and filling SS for DS is a dispensing error (slide 38; EOOR station 3), fixed by replacing the product and relabeling.'},
  {t:'Prescribing error: brand request is invalid; call the prescriber', correct:false, why:'An invalid stamp is not a prescribing error; the pharmacist simply fills the prescription as written, so no call is needed (slide 24; the Rx then allows substitution). Choosing this treats the pharmacist as responsible for correcting how the prescriber requested brand.'},
  {t:'Insufficient quantity filled', correct:false, why:'Twice daily for 10 days needs 20 tablets, and 20 were prescribed and labeled, so the quantity is right. Insufficient quantity filled is the EOOR station 9 error (60 mL when 75 mL was needed), which does not occur here.'},
  {t:'Number of refills transcribed incorrectly onto label', correct:true, why:'The prescription allows 0 refills but the label says 1. Refills must be transcribed as prescribed (slide 34, item 5), and slide 38 lists the mismatch as a transcribing error, corrected in the pharmacy system with a regenerated label (slide 39; EOOR station 4).'},
  {t:'“Use Before” date assigned on label is incorrect', correct:false, why:'One year after 11/21/2026 is 11/21/2027, which is earlier than the stock expiration 05/2028 (5/31/2028), so the label correctly uses the one-year date (slide 25). Choosing this means the stock date was copied without comparing it to the one-year limit.'}],
 teach:'Substitution is decided by how the prescriber asked for the brand, not by what the form says. Written prescriptions need the prescriber to handwrite “Brand Necessary” or “Brand Medically Necessary”; checkboxes, two-line forms, preprinted wording and rubber stamps do not prevent substitution. A verbal order needs the prescriber to say it and the pharmacist to document it; an electronic prescription needs it clearly indicated. When the request is valid, filling the generic is an error (EOOR station 2); when it is not, filling the generic is correct. Then spot the real errors: step 2 compares refills and directions with the prescription, step 3 compares strength with the bottle.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 24, 25, 34, 36–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slide 1 (stations 2, 3, 4); 8/24 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl06', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'law', concept:'star-valid-category',
 tags:['star','categories','PMR'], source:'both',
 stem:'Which prescriptions are NOT valid to fill as written? Assume the date, the patient’s name and the prescriber’s name, address and phone number are present and the drug information is complete. Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Dangerous drug: patient address and date of birth (DOB) are not on it but are in the patient medication record (PMR)', correct:false, why:'For dangerous drugs the address is not required if it is retrievable from the PMR, and the DOB is not required on the prescription if the PMR has it (slide 16; T). This prescription is valid. Choosing it applies the stricter CIII–V rule to a dangerous drug.'},
  {t:'CIV drug: patient address is not on it but is in the PMR', correct:true, why:'Schedule III–V prescriptions need the address and DOB on the prescription even when they are in the PMR (slide 19: “Even if in PMR, must have!”; T). The PMR exception is for dangerous drugs only, so this one is not valid until the prescriber supplies it.'},
  {t:'Dangerous drug: issued 14 months ago, 1 refill unused', correct:true, why:'A dangerous-drug prescription expires 1 year after issuance (slide 22). 14 months ago is about 9/2025, so the year ended about 9/2026 and the unused refill does not extend it. It cannot be filled without a new prescription. Choosing the other dangerous-drug items but not this one ignores the date.'},
  {t:'Dangerous drug: no DEA number', correct:false, why:'A DEA number is required for controlled prescriptions, not for dangerous drugs (slide 27 summary table; slide 16 lists no DEA number). This prescription is valid. Choosing it treats the DEA requirement as universal.'},
  {t:'CIII–V drug: 5 refills, issued 4 months ago, with the address, DOB and DEA number on it', correct:false, why:'Schedule III–V prescriptions allow up to 5 refills within 6 months (slide 22), and this one was issued 4 months before 11/21/2026 with every CIII–V element present (slides 18–19). It is valid. Choosing it treats a full CIII–V prescription as defective.'},
  {t:'CIII–V drug: no DEA number is written on it', correct:true, why:'Slide 18 requires the prescriber’s DEA number on CIII–V prescriptions, and the EOOR key corrects a missing DEA number by contacting the prescriber (stations 6 and 13). A missing DEA number is a prescribing error, so the prescription cannot be filled as written.'},
  {t:'Dangerous drug: 11 refills, issued 10 months ago', correct:false, why:'Dangerous drugs have no refill limit and the script lasts 1 year (slide 22), so 11 refills at 10 months is valid. Choosing this applies the CIII–V limits of 5 refills and 6 months to a dangerous drug.'},
  {t:'Written CII: the prescriber’s signature is a rubber stamp', correct:true, why:'A written CII must be on an official form and manually signed; a stamp or replica is not accepted (slide 20; slide 27, signature row; T). Choosing the other options but not this one reveals missing the signature row that is unique to CII.'}],
 teach:'Validity depends on the category, and the sources give two separate ideas: a prescription may contain more than the minimum but never less, and the pharmacy’s PMR can fill gaps only for dangerous drugs (patient address, and DOB held in the record). For CIII–V and CII the address, DOB and the prescriber’s DEA number must be on the prescription itself, the quantity is shown as a number and (if written) a word, and a written CII also needs the official form and a manual signature. Dangerous drugs need no DEA number, have no refill limit and last 1 year. Quick method: name the category first, then check the row of the slide 27 summary table that is different for that category.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 16, 18–20, 22, 27; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slides 1–2 (stations 6, 13); 8/24 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl07', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'law', concept:'star-issue-dates',
 tags:['star','expiration','date math'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Today is 11/21/2026. Based only on the date of issuance, which prescriptions can still be filled? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Alprazolam (CIV), issued 5/15/2026, 3 refills unused', correct:false, why:'A CIII–V prescription expires 6 months after issuance (slide 22). 5/15/2026 plus 6 months is 11/15/2026, so it expired 6 days ago, whatever refills remain. Choosing this means refills were allowed to extend the expiration, which they do not.'},
  {t:'Lisinopril (dangerous drug), issued 12/1/2025, 8 refills unused', correct:true, why:'A dangerous-drug prescription expires 1 year after issuance (slide 22). 12/1/2025 plus 1 year is 12/1/2026, so it is valid for 10 more days, and dangerous drugs have no refill limit. Wrongly rejecting it applies the 6-month CIII–V limit to a dangerous drug.'},
  {t:'Oxycodone (CII), issued 10/20/2026', correct:false, why:'A CII prescription expires after 30 days (slide 22; the EOOR key says more than 1 month, station 13). 10/20/2026 plus 30 days is 11/19/2026, and today is day 32 (11 days left in October + 21), so it is expired. Choosing this applies the CIII–V 6-month window to a CII.'},
  {t:'Alprazolam (CIV), issued 6/10/2026, 2 refills unused', correct:true, why:'6/10/2026 plus 6 months is 12/10/2026, so this CIV prescription is valid for 19 more days (slide 22). It was written under the same category as the 5/15 prescription, which shows that the issue date alone decides. Rejecting it applies a 30-day limit that belongs to CII.'},
  {t:'Focalin XR (CII), issued 10/28/2026', correct:true, why:'30 days after 10/28/2026 is 11/27/2026 (3 days left in October + 27), and today, 11/21/2026, is day 24 (slide 22). It is still valid, but only for 6 more days. Rejecting it applies the “expired after the previous month” instinct, ignoring that the 30 days are counted from issuance.'}],
 teach:'Count from the date of issuance, not from the date of the last fill, and do not let remaining refills extend a prescription. Dangerous drugs: 1 year, so add one year to the issue date. CIII–V: 6 months, so add six months (3 months for mid-level practitioners, slide 22 footnote). CII: 30 days, so count days (days left in the issue month, then the days into the fill month). Refills are a separate limit: none for CII, up to 5 in 6 months for CIII–V, no limit for dangerous drugs. When the date falls between two categories’ windows, such as 54 days, decide by the category, not by whether it feels old.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slide 22; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slides 1–2 (stations 6, 8, 13)'},

{id:'sl08', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'law', concept:'star-label-expiry',
 tags:['star','label date','date math'], source:'both',
 stem:'A prescription for tablets is filled on 12/20/2026 from a stock bottle with the expiration date 12/2027. Which Use Before date belongs on the prescription label?',
 options:[
  {t:'12/31/2027', correct:false, why:'12/31/2027 is the stock bottle’s expiration, because a month-and-year date runs to the end of the month (EOOR station 10). But the label takes the earlier of that date and one year after fill, which is 12/20/2027. Choosing this uses the stock date without comparing.'},
  {t:'12/20/2027', correct:true, why:'The label date is the earlier of one year after fill and the stock expiration (slide 25). One year after 12/20/2026 is 12/20/2027; the stock bottle’s 12/2027 means 12/31/2027 (month end; EOOR station 10), which is later. The earlier date, 12/20/2027, is correct.'},
  {t:'12/01/2027', correct:false, why:'This reads the stock expiration 12/2027 as the first day of the month, which is the first-of-month reading (slide 26’s option B). A month-and-year date runs to the last day (EOOR station 10; T: 2/2027 became 2/28/2027), and the one-year date is still earlier.'},
  {t:'11/30/2027', correct:false, why:'This treats a stock bottle that expires 12/2027 as expiring at the end of the previous month. The stock date 12/2027 is not earlier than one year after fill (12/20/2027), so nothing makes the label expire in November.'}],
 teach:'Use Before for tablets or capsules taken from a stock bottle is the earlier of two dates: one year after the fill date, or the stock bottle’s expiration. Write both as full dates before comparing. A stock expiration given as month and year means the last day of that month (1/2027 is 1/31/2027 in EOOR station 10, 2/2027 is 2/28/2027 in the lecture example). So a stock expiration in the same month as the one-year date is later, and the one-year date wins when the fill day is before the last day of the month; the stock date wins when it falls earlier. Constituted products such as Augmentin suspension follow the bottle’s directions instead (discard 10 days after mixing).',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 25–26; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slide 2 (station 10); 8/24 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl09', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-case-augmentin',
 tags:['star','EOOR','liquid','label date'], source:'both',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Electronic prescription (dangerous drug, complete, no brand request) for a 4-year-old child: Augmentin 400 mg/57 mg per 5 mL oral suspension, 5 mL by mouth twice daily for 10 days | Dispense: 75 mL | Indication: otitis media | Refills: 0. Label: amoxicillin-clavulanate 400 mg/57 mg per 5 mL suspension, 5 mL by mouth twice daily for 10 days, 75 mL, refills 0, Use Before 11/21/2027. The powder was mixed on 11/21/2026 and the bottle says “discard 10 days after mixing.” Which errors are present? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Insufficient quantity filled', correct:false, why:'The pharmacy filled the 75 mL that was prescribed; the shortage is in the prescription, not in the fill. Insufficient quantity filled is the EOOR station 9 error, where 60 mL was dispensed when 75 mL was needed. Choosing this confuses prescribed quantity with filled quantity.'},
  {t:'Insufficient quantity prescribed', correct:true, why:'5 mL twice daily for 10 days needs 5 × 2 × 10 = 100 mL, but 75 mL was prescribed. Slide 38 lists insufficient prescribed quantity as a prescribing error, common with liquids, so the fix is a call to the prescriber (slide 39), not a change to the fill.'},
  {t:'Incorrect drug filled: generic instead of brand', correct:false, why:'The prescription has no brand request, so substitution with the generic is allowed (slide 24), and the label correctly uses the generic name. Choosing this applies EOOR station 2 (valid brand request) to a prescription that has none.'},
  {t:'“Use Before” date assigned on label is incorrect', correct:true, why:'For a medication that is mixed, the bottle’s own directions decide the date (slide 25). 10 days after mixing on 11/21/2026 is 12/1/2026, not one year later. A label of 11/21/2027 would let the patient keep a suspension long past its life; this is a dispensing error fixed by relabeling.'},
  {t:'Directions transcribed incorrectly onto label', correct:false, why:'The label repeats 5 mL twice daily for 10 days, so the directions match the prescription (slide 34, item 3). The directions are right but the amount prescribed is not enough to carry them out, which is a prescribing issue.'},
  {t:'Incorrect strength/concentration filled', correct:false, why:'The label and prescription both say 400 mg/57 mg per 5 mL, and slide 36 asks only whether the strength matches; nothing in the case says the bottle differs. Choosing this guesses an error where the concentration is consistent.'}],
 teach:'Two checks are specific to liquids. First, do the arithmetic: dose per administration × times per day × days must equal the quantity prescribed (5 mL × 2 × 10 = 100 mL), and slide 38 calls a short amount prescribed a prescribing error (call the prescriber), unlike a short amount filled, which is a dispensing error. Second, constituted products do not use the one-year or stock-bottle rule; check the bottle’s directions (discard 10 days after mixing) and count the days from the day it is mixed (11/21/2026 + 10 days = 12/1/2026). The two errors have different fixes: one goes to the prescriber, the other is corrected by relabeling.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 24–25, 34, 36–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slide 1 (station 9); 8/24 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl10', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-counsel-rubric',
 tags:['star','counseling','rubric'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacy intern counsels a patient who just picked up a new prescription. Which of the intern’s questions does NOT meet the counseling rubric?',
 options:[
  {t:'“What did your doctor tell you this new medication was going to do for you?”', correct:false, why:'This is prime question #1 in the rubric’s words (indication; slides 43–44, item 9). It is open-ended and asks what the patient already knows. Choosing it means mistaking the recommended opening question for a defect.'},
  {t:'“Can you tell me, in your own words, how you plan to take this medication?”', correct:false, why:'This is a teach-back question: rubric item 27 asks the pharmacist to verify understanding and feedback (slide 44). It is open-ended and meets the rubric. Choosing it confuses teach-back with a leading question.'},
  {t:'“Do you have allergies, are you pregnant, and what other medicines do you take?”', correct:true, why:'Rubric item 26 asks for open-ended questions and to avoid compounded or leading ones (slide 44). Allergies, pregnancy or breastfeeding and other medicines are separate items (4, 5, 6), so each is asked on its own. Bundling three questions into one fails the rubric.'},
  {t:'“What did your doctor tell you to expect once you start taking this medication?”', correct:false, why:'This is prime question #3 (slide 43; rubric item 18), which opens the discussion of side effects, expected benefit and warnings (items 19–21). Choosing it confuses a required prime question with an unacceptable one.'}],
 teach:'The 3 prime questions are what the doctor told the patient the medication is for, how to take it, and what to expect (slide 43). In the rubric they are items 9, 11 and 18, each followed by the pharmacist’s own information: indication (10), regimen (12) and then side effects, expectation and warnings (19–21). The rubric also scores communication: open-ended questions, no compounded or leading questions (26), no medical jargon (25), teach-back (27) and closure (28). Quick method: when an item asks three things at once, or suggests the answer (“this is for your blood pressure, right?”), it fails item 26. Allergies, pregnancy or breastfeeding and other medicines are each required, but as separate questions.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 43–44'},

{id:'sl11', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'rx', concept:'star-unsafe-dose',
 tags:['star','EOOR','safety','prescribing error'], source:'both',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Electronic prescription (dangerous drug, complete): issued 11/20/2026 | ibuprofen 800 mg tablets, #120, 1 tablet by mouth every 4 hours as needed for pain | Refills: 0. A drug reference gives the maximum daily dose as 3200 mg. Label: ibuprofen 800 mg tablets, #120, 1 tablet by mouth every 4 hours as needed for pain, refills 0, Use Before 11/21/2027. Stock bottle: ibuprofen 800 mg tablets, Exp 09/2028. What is the best assessment and action?',
 options:[
  {t:'Prescribing error: dose too high; call the prescriber', correct:true, why:'Every 4 hours is up to 6 tablets, 6 × 800 = 4800 mg a day, above the 3200 mg maximum. Step 1 asks whether the prescription is safe (slide 32; T), and a dose that is too high is a prescribing error (slide 38) fixed by contacting the prescriber with a recommendation (EOOR station 7).'},
  {t:'Transcribing error: re-enter the sig as every 6 hours and regenerate the label', correct:false, why:'The label matches the prescription, so there is no transcription mistake, and the pharmacist cannot rewrite the sig alone. Slide 39 corrects transcribing errors only when the entry differs from the prescription. Choosing this changes the prescriber’s order without calling.'},
  {t:'Dispensing error: replace the 800 mg stock with 600 mg tablets and relabel', correct:false, why:'The bottle matches the prescription (800 mg, correct drug and formulation), so the product is not the problem. Slide 39 replaces the product only for dispensing errors. Choosing this changes the strength to lower the dose, which is the prescriber’s decision.'},
  {t:'No errors and safe to dispense as is; the prescription, label and bottle agree', correct:false, why:'The three documents agree, but agreement covers only steps 2 and 3. Step 1 also asks whether the prescription is safe, and (T) the pharmacist is expected to check Lexicomp or Micromedex. Choosing this stops after the match check and skips the safety check.'}],
 teach:'Legal is not the same as safe. Step 1 of the review asks both, and the first question the pharmacist asks about a dose is whether the amount per day is within the reference maximum (Lexicomp or Micromedex; (T) you are not expected to know the drug by memory, but to look it up). Compute the daily total: doses per day × strength. Here every 4 hours is 6 doses, 6 × 800 mg = 4800 mg, above 3200 mg. A prescribed dose or frequency that is too high or too low is a prescribing error; the correction is to contact the prescriber with a new recommendation (EOOR station 7: 7200 mg a day as prescribed, maximum 3200). A technician cannot fix it, and the label matching the prescription does not make it safe.',
 cite:'Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 30, 32, 38–39; EOOR-Answer-Key.pdf slide 1 (station 7); 8/24 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl12', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-quest-establish',
 tags:['star','QuEST','acne','SCHOLAR-MAC'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 17-year-old boy asks the pharmacist for an OTC acne product. The pharmacist uses QuEST: Quickly assess, Establish self-care candidacy, Suggest self-care strategies, Talk about the product. Which actions belong to the Establish self-care candidacy step? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Asks when the acne began and what makes it worse', correct:false, why:'Onset and aggravating factors are SCHOLAR-MAC elements collected in the first step, Quickly assess. Establishing candidacy uses those answers; it does not collect them. Choosing this mixes the first and second QuEST steps.'},
  {t:'Decides he is not a self-care candidate because of nodular lesions with moderate to extensive scarring', correct:true, why:'Severe acne, with nodular lesions and moderate to extensive scarring (slide 8), is an exclusion from self-care (slide 18), so this refer-or-treat decision is the Establish step. Missing it means treating a severe case with an OTC product instead of referring.'},
  {t:'Advises washing twice a day with a mild cleanser and no abrasives', correct:false, why:'Cleansing twice a day without abrasives is a non-drug self-care strategy (Patel slide 19), so it belongs to Suggest self-care strategies. Choosing it confuses a strategy with the candidacy decision.'},
  {t:'Decides the patient is not a self-care candidate because a corticosteroid on the list can cause the acne', correct:true, why:'Exacerbating factors and comedogenic drugs exclude a patient from self-care (slide 18), and slide 10 names corticosteroids among the acne-causing drugs. Making this decision is the Establish step. Collecting the medication list itself is the first step.'},
  {t:'Tells him to pat the skin dry and wait 20–30 minutes before applying the gel', correct:false, why:'Application instructions belong to the last step, Talk about the product (Patel slide 42, administration). Choosing this mixes the final counseling step with the candidacy decision.'},
  {t:'Decides rosacea is possible, so he is not a self-care candidate', correct:true, why:'Possible rosacea is another exclusion from self-care (slide 18), so ruling it in or out helps establish candidacy. Missing it means using acne OTC treatment where a prescriber should see the patient first.'}],
 teach:'QuEST steps in order: Q, quickly assess with SCHOLAR-MAC (collects onset, location, aggravating and remitting factors, medications, allergies, conditions); E, establish self-care candidacy (decide whether the patient is an appropriate candidate); S, suggest self-care strategies (non-drug measures, product selection); T, talk about the selected product (administration, efficacy, safety). For acne, the Establish step uses the exclusions on Patel slide 18: severe acne, possible rosacea, exacerbating factors and comedogenic drugs. Quick method: a question or fact gathered from the patient is Q, a refer-or-treat decision is E, advice about habits or product choice is S, and how to use the product is T.',
 cite:'DEENT-Lab-Deck.pdf, QuEST/SCHOLAR-MAC and Part 1 slides; Patel_Acne-Therapeutics-Student.pdf slides 10, 18, 19, 42; 11/16 transcript (T, QuEST steps)'},

{id:'sl13', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-acne-next-step',
 tags:['star','acne','pathway'], source:'both',
 note:'The 12-year tetracycline limit follows Dr. Patel’s slide 33; product labeling for individual tetracyclines may set a different age.',
 stem:'A 17-year-old boy with moderate acne has used benzoyl peroxide each morning and adapalene each night as directed for 12 weeks. His lesion count has not fallen and new inflamed lesions keep appearing. He takes no other medicines. What is the best recommendation?',
 options:[
  {t:'Continue the same two products alone for 8 more weeks', correct:false, why:'Visible benefit takes 8 weeks or more and a 10–25% fall in lesion count is expected within 4–8 weeks (slides 41, 44). After 12 weeks with no change the trial is complete. Choosing this repeats the “be patient” advice that applies to the first weeks.'},
  {t:'Refer for a prescriber to add an oral antibiotic to the topicals', correct:true, why:'Moderate acne starts with a topical combination, and after the topical combinations have been tried an oral antibiotic is added (T, Dr. Patel’s stepwise order; slide 40 lists oral regimens for moderate acne). At 17 he is over the 12-year tetracycline limit (slide 33), and the topicals continue.'},
  {t:'Stop both topical products and refer for an oral antibiotic by itself', correct:false, why:'Slide 40 gives an oral antibiotic in combination with topical agents, not alone, and (T) Dr. Patel said the oral antibiotic is given with the topicals. Choosing this drops the topicals that keep the regimen working.'},
  {t:'Switch to a salicylic acid cleanser, which has stronger evidence', correct:false, why:'Salicylic acid has limited efficacy data, and benzoyl peroxide is preferred (slide 29). It would be a step down from two products already tried. Choosing this reveals treating an older first-line agent as stronger than a topical combination.'}],
 teach:'Dr. Patel’s order for acne (T) is stepwise: mild acne starts with one topical agent (benzoyl peroxide or a topical retinoid), then the other, then a topical combination; moderate acne starts with the combination of benzoyl peroxide plus a topical retinoid; an oral antibiotic is added to the topicals when topical combinations have been tried; severe acne is not a self-care candidate. Keep two numbers: 8 weeks or more before visible benefit, and a 10–25% drop in lesion count by 4–8 weeks. Tetracyclines are for patients over 12 years, not in pregnancy, not with isotretinoin, for the shortest possible duration with re-evaluation at 3–4 months (slide 33). The pharmacist refers because an oral antibiotic needs a prescriber.',
 note:'Slide 40 lists an oral antibiotic plus a topical agent as a moderate first-line option; Dr. Patel’s order (T, 11/10 transcript) puts the oral antibiotic after the topical combination, and this item follows her order.',
 cite:'Patel_Acne-Therapeutics-Student.pdf slides 29, 33, 40–41, 44; 11/10 transcript (T, Dr. Patel’s order)'},

{id:'sl14', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-adapalene-counsel',
 tags:['star','acne','counseling','adapalene'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 15-year-old with mild acne will start OTC adapalene 0.1% gel and keeps using the benzoyl peroxide wash he already has. Which counseling points are correct? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Use the adapalene at night and the benzoyl peroxide in the morning', correct:true, why:'Slide 27 says the OTC retinoid is applied at night and kept separate from benzoyl peroxide (am versus pm). Splitting the two products keeps both in the regimen. Missing this means advising both at the same time, the frequent mistake.'},
  {t:'Add a salicylic acid cleanser at the same time for added effect', correct:false, why:'Slide 27 says not to use the retinoid with sulfur, resorcinol, salicylic acid or vitamin C products (T: vitamin C only if kept separate). Salicylic acid is also less effective than benzoyl peroxide (slide 29). Choosing this stacks products that should not be combined.'},
  {t:'Wash with a mild soap, pat dry, wait 20–30 minutes, then apply a thin layer', correct:true, why:'Slide 42 gives this administration sequence (thin layer, pat dry, wait 20–30 min) for topical acne products: gentle wash, pat dry, wait 20–30 minutes, thin layer to the affected area, avoiding the eyes, ears, nostrils and mouth. The thin layer goes only on the affected area.'},
  {t:'Expect it to clear acne in 1 week; stop it if the skin looks the same', correct:false, why:'Slide 41 says visible benefit takes 8 weeks or more, and a 10–25% drop in lesion count is expected within 4–8 weeks (slide 44). Choosing this sets an expectation far too short and invites stopping the product before it can work.'},
  {t:'Use a moisturizer and sunscreen alongside it', correct:true, why:'Slide 27 advises a moisturizer and sunscreen with adapalene, as part of the counseling for this retinoid. Missing this leaves out a point the counseling step expects, and it is easy to forget when the focus is on timing.'},
  {t:'Apply a thick layer to damp skin right after washing so it absorbs better', correct:false, why:'Slide 42 calls for a thin layer after patting dry and waiting 20–30 minutes, so applying right after washing and using a thick layer both depart from that sequence. Choosing this repeats a common misconception that more product or wetter skin works better.'}],
 teach:'In the Talk step for an OTC retinoid: age, timing, interactions, administration and expectations. Adapalene 0.1% is for patients over 12 years (slide 27); applied at night; benzoyl peroxide goes in the morning; moisturizer and sunscreen daily; not combined with sulfur, resorcinol, salicylic acid or vitamin C products. Administration (slide 42): wash gently with a mild soap, pat dry, wait 20–30 minutes, apply a thin layer to the affected area, and avoid the eyes, ears, nostrils and mouth. Expectation (slides 41, 44): 8 weeks or more for visible benefit, and a 10–25% drop in lesion count by 4–8 weeks. If stinging or irritation develops, temporarily stop or use it less often.',
 cite:'Patel_Acne-Therapeutics-Student.pdf slides 27, 29, 41–42, 44; 11/10 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl15', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-allergic-exclusion',
 tags:['star','ophthalmic','conjunctivitis','referral'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 28-year-old woman has itchy, tearing, red eyes every spring. Today both eyes are worse, and she reports light sensitivity and aching pain in her right eye. She asks for the antihistamine eye drops she used last year. What is the best response?',
 options:[
  {t:'Refer her; pain and light sensitivity exclude self-treatment', correct:true, why:'Itch and tearing point to allergic conjunctivitis (slides 28, 29), but eye pain and light sensitivity are exclusions from self-treatment (slides 11, 30). Two steps decide the answer: classify, then check the exclusions. When an exclusion is present, referral replaces the OTC regimen.'},
  {t:'Recommend cold compresses and watch for improvement', correct:false, why:'Cold compresses fit allergic conjunctivitis, but watching and waiting is only for patients who are candidates for self-treatment. With aching pain and light sensitivity she is excluded (slides 11, 30), so this delays the eye exam she needs.'},
  {t:'Recommend ketotifen drops alone and watch for improvement', correct:false, why:'Ketotifen is the lab key’s example medication for allergic conjunctivitis, and slide 35 gives a dual-acting agent for that condition. This patient is excluded by aching pain and light sensitivity (slides 11, 30). Choosing this treats the itch and misses the red flags.'},
  {t:'Recommend artificial tears plus ketotifen, the first-line OTC pair', correct:false, why:'Slide 35 gives artificial tears plus an ophthalmic dual-acting agent (ketotifen per the lab key) for allergic conjunctivitis, but only when the patient can be self-treated. Choosing this applies the right diagnosis and ignores the exclusions that send her to a provider.'}],
 teach:'The same exclusion list applies to dry eye and conjunctivitis: eye pain, ocular trauma, chemical exposure, heat exposure (not sun exposure), blurred vision not due to eye ointments, light sensitivity, and signs of eyelid infection other than a stye. Check it before choosing a product. After you classify (itch means allergic; lid crusting without itch means bacterial or viral; gritty with a normal or mildly red eye means dry eye; no symptoms until field loss means open-angle glaucoma), the exclusions can still override the product. For an eligible allergic patient the first-line OTC pair is artificial tears plus a dual-acting agent (slide 35), with ketotifen as the lab key’s example.',
 cite:'Lam_OphthalmicDisorders.pdf slides 11, 28–30, 35; Ophthalmic-Review-Key.pdf'},

{id:'sl16', skill:'tell', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-eye-grid-pairs',
 tags:['star','ophthalmic','Which is which','sorting'], source:'both',
 stem:'In the ophthalmic review (dry eye disease, allergic conjunctivitis, bacterial or viral conjunctivitis, primary open-angle glaucoma), which pairings agree with the lab’s answer key? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Warm compress: dry eye disease', correct:true, why:'The Compress row of the key gives a warm compress for dry eye, a cold compress for the two conjunctivitis types and nothing for glaucoma. Missing it reverses the compress row, placing the warm compress with a conjunctivitis type.'},
  {t:'Cold compress: primary open-angle glaucoma', correct:false, why:'The Compress row says a compress is not helpful for open-angle glaucoma, because treatment targets intraocular pressure. Choosing this applies the compress idea to a condition where it has no role.'},
  {t:'Ketotifen twice daily, age 3 and older: bacterial or viral conjunctivitis', correct:false, why:'The Example medication row gives ketotifen for allergic conjunctivitis. Choosing it for bacterial or viral conjunctivitis confuses the itch-driven antihistamine with the antimicrobial in that column.'},
  {t:'Cold compress: allergic conjunctivitis', correct:true, why:'The Compress row gives a cold compress for allergic conjunctivitis (and for bacterial or viral conjunctivitis), and a warm compress for dry eye. Missing it means mixing the two compress types between dry eye and allergy.'},
  {t:'Example medication for allergic conjunctivitis: artificial tears once or twice a day on arising and before retiring', correct:false, why:'In the key’s Example medication row, artificial tears once or twice a day on arising and before retiring is the dry eye cell, and ketotifen is the allergic cell. The First line row for allergic conjunctivitis also begins with artificial tears, but that is a different row from this one.'},
  {t:'Ciprofloxacin ophthalmic every 2 hours for 2 days, then every 4 hours for 5 days: bacterial or viral conjunctivitis', correct:true, why:'The key gives ciprofloxacin ophthalmic solution 1–2 drops every 2 hours for 2 days, then every 4 hours for 5 days, as the bacterial or viral example. Missing it means missing that this column’s first line is an ocular antimicrobial.'}],
 teach:'The Which is which grid has six rows by four conditions. Presentation: dry eye, mildly red eye with a sandy gritty feeling; allergic, itching, tearing and red eyes; bacterial or viral, tearing with lid crusting; open-angle glaucoma, no symptoms until substantial visual field loss. Compress: dry eye warm, allergic cold, bacterial or viral cold, glaucoma none. First line: dry eye lubricants, allergic tears then antihistamines, bacterial or viral an ocular antimicrobial with referral where applicable, glaucoma prostaglandin analogs. Examples: artificial tears (dry eye), ketotifen twice daily from age 3 (allergic), ciprofloxacin ophthalmic 2 days every 2 hours then 5 days every 4 hours, latanoprost once daily. Learn it by column, then check each row.',
 note:'The key lists an ocular antimicrobial as first line for bacterial or viral conjunctivitis; Dr. Lam’s slide 34 gives cold compresses and referral. These pairings follow the lab’s key.',
 cite:'Ophthalmic-Review-Key.pdf (Compress row; Example medication row; First line row)'},

{id:'sl17', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-glaucoma-case',
 tags:['star','EOOR','eye drops','glaucoma'], source:'both',
 stem:'Fill date 11/21/2026. Electronic prescription (dangerous drug, complete), issued 11/18/2026 | Lumigan (bimatoprost) 0.01% ophthalmic solution, 1 drop in both eyes (OU) once daily at bedtime, for primary open-angle glaucoma | Refills: 5. Label: bimatoprost 0.01% ophthalmic solution, 1 drop in both eyes twice daily, refills 5. Stock bottle: Latisse (bimatoprost), the eyelash-growth product. Which errors are present? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Prescribed dose/frequency is too high', correct:false, why:'The prescription says once daily, which is the expected frequency for a prostaglandin analog (Lam_Glaucoma slide 20). The twice-daily frequency appears only on the label, so the fault is in transcribing. Choosing this blames the prescriber for the label.'},
  {t:'Directions/sig transcribed incorrectly onto label', correct:true, why:'The prescription says once daily and the label says twice daily. Step 2 checks the sig as prescribed (slide 34, item 3), and slide 38 lists it as a transcribing error, corrected in the pharmacy system with a regenerated label (slide 39). More frequent dosing also decreases efficacy (T).'},
  {t:'Incorrect drug entered/transcribed', correct:false, why:'The label names bimatoprost 0.01%, the drug that was prescribed, so the drug was entered correctly. The product mismatch is in the bottle, not in the entry. Choosing this puts a dispensing mistake at the transcription step.'},
  {t:'Incorrect drug filled', correct:true, why:'Bimatoprost is sold as Lumigan for glaucoma and as Latisse for eyelash growth (Lam_Glaucoma slide 19); the bottle is Latisse, so the product does not match what was prescribed (slides 36–37). This is a dispensing error fixed by replacing the product and relabeling before dispensing (slide 39).'},
  {t:'Incorrect formulation filled', correct:false, why:'Formulation is tablet versus capsule, IR versus ER, or suspension versus solution (slides 36, 38). Both products are ophthalmic solutions of the same drug, so the difference is the product and its use, not the formulation.'},
  {t:'Prescription is expired', correct:false, why:'A dangerous-drug prescription lasts 1 year (slide 22), and this one was issued 3 days before the fill. Choosing it applies a shorter category limit and ignores the issue date, which the case gives.'}],
 teach:'Glaucoma prescriptions are reviewed with the same three steps as any prescription. The eye-drop facts that make errors checkable: prostaglandin analogs (latanoprost, bimatoprost) are used once daily, and more frequent dosing decreases efficacy; dorzolamide alone is TID and with timolol (Cosopt) BID; netarsudil is once at bedtime; bimatoprost is Lumigan for glaucoma and Latisse for eyelash growth. A mismatch between prescription and label is a transcribing error (re-enter the sig, regenerate the label). A mismatch between label and bottle is a dispensing error (replace the product, relabel). A wrong frequency written by the prescriber is a prescribing error (call the prescriber).',
 cite:'Lam_Glaucoma.pdf slides 19, 20, 24–25 (bank); Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slides 22, 34, 36–39'},

{id:'sl18', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-cosopt-sbar',
 tags:['star','EOOR','eye drops','SBAR'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A prescription reads Cosopt (dorzolamide/timolol), 1 drop in both eyes (OU) once daily. The label and the stock bottle both match the prescription. The pharmacist calls the prescriber using SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation). Which Recommendation is correct?',
 options:[
  {t:'Change to 1 drop in both eyes three times daily', correct:false, why:'Dorzolamide alone (Trusopt) is TID (Lam_Glaucoma slide 51), but Cosopt also contains timolol and is BID (slide 69). Choosing this applies the single-agent frequency to the combination product, the exact confusion on the slide 69 table.'},
  {t:'Change to 1 drop in both eyes twice daily', correct:true, why:'Cosopt is BID (slide 69; the combinations shown there are BID or TID). The error is a prescribing error, not a label or bottle error, so the pharmacist calls the prescriber (slide 38) and the Recommendation asks for twice daily.'},
  {t:'Change to 1 drop in both eyes once at bedtime', correct:false, why:'Netarsudil (Rhopressa) is the once-at-bedtime product (slide 62); a dorzolamide/timolol combination is not dosed that way. Choosing this takes the once-daily idea from a different class instead of from the drug named.'},
  {t:'Keep 1 drop in both eyes once daily', correct:false, why:'Timoptic XE gel is the timolol product given once daily, but Cosopt is a combination solution, and the combinations shown on slide 69 are BID or TID. Choosing this carries the gel’s frequency to a different product.'}],
 teach:'When the prescription, label and bottle all match but the prescribed frequency is wrong for the drug, the error is in the prescribing step and the fix is a call to the prescriber, structured as SBAR: Situation (who you are and the patient), Background (the prescription as written), Assessment (what is wrong, for example that this combination is dosed twice daily), Recommendation (the specific change). Frequencies that distinguish neighbors: dorzolamide alone TID; dorzolamide plus timolol BID; timolol solution BID; timolol gel-forming solution (Timoptic XE) once daily; prostaglandin analogs once daily; netarsudil once at bedtime.',
 cite:'Lam_Glaucoma.pdf slides 32, 51, 62, 69; DEENT-Lab-Deck.pdf, Part 2 slide; Intro-to-Dispensing.pdf slide 38'},

{id:'sl19', skill:'tell', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-sd-vignette',
 tags:['star','seborrheic dermatitis','sorting'], source:'both',
 stem:'A man has yellow, greasy scales on red skin of the scalp and face. Which statements about his condition and OTC care are correct? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Ketoconazole shampoo is the first-line choice for the scalp', correct:true, why:'The greasy, yellowish scales with redness on the scalp and face are seborrheic dermatitis, and ketoconazole shampoo is first line (slide 16; T). Missing it means choosing a second-line agent such as selenium sulfide, pyrithione zinc or coal tar.'},
  {t:'Hydrocortisone 1% is not effective for his condition', correct:false, why:'Slide 7 gives hydrocortisone as not effective for dandruff, but used for seborrheic dermatitis and psoriasis (T). Choosing this applies the dandruff “no” to a patient with seborrheic dermatitis.'},
  {t:'Inflammation and epidermal hyperplasia are present', correct:true, why:'Seborrheic dermatitis and psoriasis both have inflammation and epidermal hyperplasia, which dandruff lacks (slide 6). Slide 8 words dandruff’s inflammation as mild, but slide 6 keys it absent, so the sorting follows slide 6. Missing it means calling this dandruff.'},
  {t:'Sulfur is not effective for his condition', correct:false, why:'Slide 7 shows sulfur as effective for dandruff and seborrheic dermatitis; its “no” cell is for psoriasis only. Choosing this misplaces the sulfur “no”, the exact neighbor confusion with hydrocortisone.'},
  {t:'Malassezia fungi are involved', correct:true, why:'Dandruff and seborrheic dermatitis involve Malassezia; psoriasis is an immune-mediated disease (slides 8, 11, 12, 21). This is why an antifungal shampoo is first line. Missing it means grouping this condition with psoriasis.'},
  {t:'He should see a provider only if it persists longer than 3 weeks', correct:false, why:'Slide 16 sends the patient to a health care provider if the disorder worsens or persists longer than 7 days. Choosing 3 weeks lengthens the referral point past the 7 days given for seborrheic dermatitis.'}],
 teach:'Sort the three scaly dermatoses by key words: dandruff is thin white or grey flakes with no inflammation; seborrheic dermatitis is greasy, yellowish scales on red skin, with inflammation and epidermal hyperplasia, on the scalp, face and trunk (slide 6); psoriasis is sharp-bordered red plaques with silvery white scales on the elbows, knees, scalp and trunk. Malassezia is involved in dandruff and seborrheic dermatitis but not psoriasis. In the OTC ingredient grid, only two cells say no: hydrocortisone for dandruff, and sulfur for psoriasis. Seborrheic dermatitis first line is ketoconazole shampoo; consult a provider if worse or longer than 7 days.',
 cite:'Patel_Scaly-Dermatoses-Therapeutics-Student.pdf slides 6–8, 11–12, 16, 21; 11/10 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sl20', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Lahoud', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starl', sub:'deent', concept:'star-psoriasis-bsa',
 tags:['star','psoriasis','BSA','sorting'], source:'both',
 stem:'Psoriasis is classified by the percent of body surface area (BSA) involved. When a value falls between two cut-offs, give both classes. Which classifications are correct? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'BSA 5%: mild', correct:true, why:'Mild psoriasis is a BSA of 5% or less (slides 26, 31). 5% sits on the mild cut-off. Missing it means treating the limit as moderate, instead of counting the cut-off itself as mild.'},
  {t:'BSA 4%: mild to moderate', correct:false, why:'A BSA of 4% is at or below the 5% cut-off, so it is mild (slides 26, 31). The two-name label is used only when the value falls between two cut-offs (T). Choosing it overuses the in-between label.'},
  {t:'BSA 7%: mild to moderate', correct:true, why:'7% is above the mild cut-off (5%) and below the moderate cut-off (8%), so it takes both names (slides 26, 31; T: 11/11 transcript). Missing it means forcing a value between two cut-offs into one class.'},
  {t:'BSA 8%: mild to moderate', correct:false, why:'The moderate cut-off is 8% or more (slides 26, 31), so 8% is moderate. Choosing mild to moderate treats the cut-off itself as an in-between value, when the two-name label is reserved for numbers that fall between cut-offs (T: 11/11 transcript).'},
  {t:'BSA 9%: moderate to severe', correct:true, why:'9% is above the moderate cut-off (8%) and below the severe cut-off (10%), so under the two-name convention stated in the stem it is moderate to severe. Slides 26 and 31 give only the cut-offs; the pairing comes from the 11/11 transcript (T). Missing it means choosing one class alone.'},
  {t:'BSA 12%: moderate to severe', correct:false, why:'Severe psoriasis is a BSA of 10% or more (slides 26, 31), so 12% is severe. Dr. Patel first said 20% and then told students to follow the slide’s 10% (T). Choosing the two-name label here keeps a value that is already past the severe cut-off.'}],
 teach:'BSA cut-offs: mild 5% or less, moderate 8% or more, severe 10% or more. A value between two cut-offs takes both names: above 5% and below 8% is mild to moderate (for example 7%), above 8% and below 10% is moderate to severe (for example 9%). A value on a cut-off takes that class: 5% mild, 8% moderate, 10% severe. Some phototherapy trials use 20% or more as the lower limit for severe, which is why Dr. Patel mentioned 20%; for this course use the slide’s 10% (T). Quick method: draw the number line with 5, 8 and 10, mark where the value falls, and name the class or the pair of classes.',
 note:'Slides 26 and 31 key mild at 5% or less, moderate at 8% or more and severe at 10% or more. The two-name labels for in-between values (7% and 9%) come from the 11/11 transcript (T), so the stem states that convention.',
 cite:'Patel_Scaly-Dermatoses-Therapeutics-Student.pdf slides 26, 31, 50; 11/11 transcript (T)'}
);
